[ ] 3. Golden test on the GPU: pytest tests/test_phase0b_golden.py
      Passing = the refactor changed no numbers. If values differ: check that both sublayers
      read and write through the residual, the order of the additions, and which norm feeds which.

[ ] 4. post_step: the router bias update leaves forward
      What: each MoE router keeps one bias per expert that nudges tokens toward under-used
        experts; it is adjusted from how many tokens each expert received. That adjustment should
        happen once per optimizer step, but forward runs a variable number of times per step
        (evaluation, gradient accumulation, activation recomputation). So forward only records
        the counts, and post_step(), called once per step, applies them.

      Contract:
        MoE.forward: when self.training, add this batch's expert counts [E] into a non-persistent
          buffer, and T * top_k into a running Python int (a plain int avoids a GPU sync).
          No bias update in forward any more.
        MoE.post_step() -> None: if anything was accumulated: f = counts / total;
          self.router.update_expert_bias(f); reset both. Nothing accumulated -> no-op.
          Eval forwards never accumulate.
        TransformerLM.post_step() -> None: calls post_step() on every block's ffn.
        train_step: calls model.post_step() once, after muon.step() and adamw.step().
      Same training result as today with one forward per step: the bias only affects which
        experts are selected, and selection passes no gradient.
      Files: model/moe/moe.py, model/model.py, training/train_step.py

[ ] 5. Full suite on the GPU, then tell Claude. Claude updates the tests that fail only because of
      renamed attributes or new signatures, marks D15 Implemented, and replaces this checklist
      with step 3's.

